%description:

Tcp.pushSegmentsOnWriteBoundary:write-end -- the segment that ends a write has PSH.

The claim (Tcp.ned): "set PSH as Linux: on the segment that ends a write without MSG_MORE
(tcp_mark_push()) [...]".

The classes: off (false, the default), and two facets of on (true; LinuxTcp): write-end, PSH
on the segment that ends a write; long-write, PSH also inside a write longer than half the
largest window. The code: TcpConnection::sendSegment() and the marks of a SEND
(enqueueSendCommandData()). This test separates the facet from "off".

Host under test: host1, which writes 1448 bytes two times during its handshake. Observation:
level 1, the first data segment, the end of the first write, has PSH.

%# parameter: Tcp.pushSegmentsOnWriteBoundary
%# class: write-end
%# value: true
%# key: *.host1.tcp.pushSegmentsOnWriteBoundary
%# expected-result: PASS

%includes:
#include "ProtocolTest.h"
#include "TcpTestApp.h"

%global:

using namespace inet;
using namespace inet::protocoltest;

Define_ProtocolTestProgram()
{
    return ProtocolTest("TcpPushSegmentsOnWriteBoundaryWriteEnd")
        .act(action().at(0.1).run<TcpTestApp>("host1.app[0]", [](TcpTestApp& app) {
            app.socket().connect(app.addressOf("host2"), 1000);
            app.sendData(app.socket(), 1448);
            app.sendData(app.socket(), 1448);
        }))
        .once(on("host1.tcp").signal("packetSentToLower")
             .filterPacket("tcp.synBit == false && pk.dataLength > tcp.headerLength")
             .first()
                  .assertPacket("tcp.pshBit == true")
             .describe("the first data segment, with PSH")
             .within(0.5));
}

%activity:
EV << "TcpPushSegmentsOnWriteBoundaryWriteEnd compiled\n";

%inifile: omnetpp.ini

[General]
*.host1.tcp.pushSegmentsOnWriteBoundary = true    # the parameter under test
*.host1.app[0].typename = "TcpTestApp"
include ../../parameter/ini/_bulk.ini
include ../../parameter/ini/_base.ini

%contains: stdout
PROTOCOLTEST TcpPushSegmentsOnWriteBoundaryWriteEnd: PASS
